[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | symbol | C `nm` class | Rust |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |--------|--------------|------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `helperBad`  | `t` (local) | private `fn helperBad` (`#[allow(dead_code)]`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `helperGood` | `t` (local) | private `fn helperGood` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note: `helperBad` is dead code in the C too — `bad()` never calls it. That is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduced faithfully; see `CONFIGS.md` row 12.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Undefined symbols
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C `.so` imports only `puts` (GCC rewrites `printf("%s\n", s)` into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `puts(s)`), plus the standard weak ELF/glibc stubs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust `.so` imports `puts` as well — LLVM applies the identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%s\n", s)` → `puts(s)` transformation to the `c_printf` call in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printLine`. The remaining Rust imports are all libc / libgcc unwinder symbols
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pulled in by the Rust standard library (`malloc`, `memcpy`, `write`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `_Unwind_*`, `pthread_key_*`, …).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Non-libc undefined symbols in the Rust `.so`: 0.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every undefined symbol resolves against `libc.so.6`, `libgcc_s.so.1`, or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`, all of which are listed as `NEEDED`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Note on `SONAME`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C `.so` carries `SONAME = libdriver.so`; the Rust `cdylib` carries no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SONAME`. Both files are also *named* `libdriver.so`. Because the differential
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests `dlopen` both objects into a single process, the test suite asserts (in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `test_00_both_libraries_are_distinct_objects`) that the two handles resolve
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` to different addresses, guarding against the loader silently aliasing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the second `dlopen` to the first object — which would make every comparison
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m trivially self-consistent and worthless.
